WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.160
Have you seen the news lately?

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.500
It sucks.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.739
Today’s media landscape gives people new
ways to hear only the things they want to

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:54.739
hear.

00:00:54.739 --> 00:00:59.629
When confronted with ideas that challenge
their beliefs, many react with outrage, censorship

00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:03.260
– and recently, cries of “fake news.”

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.720
This trend may seem to have popped out of
nowhere, but this was actually predicted a

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.630
long time ago in a book you’ve probably
read: Ray Bradbury’s Fahrenheit 451.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:18.119
In case you slept through middle school English,
the book depicts a dark future where ‘firemen’

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:23.070
have graduated from saving cats to torching
books in an attempt to silence any troubling

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.060
ideas.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:29.060
Fahrenheit is usually interpreted as an attack
on government censorship—especially since

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.130
Bradbury wrote the book in the midst of McCarthy’s
War on Communism.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.740
But there’s actually someone else Bradbury
blames for the dystopia his characters live

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.820
in: YOU!

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.820
Well not only you, but all of us.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:48.009
See, in the book, the main character is informed
that it was the PUBLIC that censored themselves.

00:01:48.009 --> 00:01:50.759
It all started with the advent of mass media.

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:54.700
Technology advanced to such a degree that
people struggled to keep up.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.820
Information rushed in from every direction,
overwhelming the population.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.939
To quell their anxiety, people focused on
smaller pieces of information.

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:07.869
Beatty, captain of the firefighters, describes
this change – how media’s been reduced

00:02:07.869 --> 00:02:11.090
from cumbersome novels to “the gag, the
snap ending”.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:13.950
“One column, two sentences, a headline!

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.250
Then mid-air, all vanishes...

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:19.860
Just like that, we consume and forget and
consume again.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.000
Rinse, wash, repeat.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.260
The mind drinks less and less”

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:29.590
Soon, people became addicts, conditioned to
feel pleasure from frivolous content.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.320
Anything thought-provoking became downright
painful.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.110
As a result, people censored themselves from
any challenging ideas.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:41.420
They sought to be pacified, numbed, and validated
– soundbites, factoids, and vapid drama

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:44.350
blaring out any troubling ideas.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.340
Blaring out reality.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:51.230
We see similar patterns with the rise of social
networking and the “Feed”- News story

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:57.810
after news story that generates as you scroll
– video after video, meme after meme directly

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:03.780
fed and programmed specifically to your liking—just
as long as you continue scrolling.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.690
In Fahrenheit, the media concluded that it
could attract the biggest audience by ridding

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:13.410
itself of anything controversial, effectively
ceding control of its content to outrage.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:18.370
Our media-saturated society is an extension
of Bradbury’s vision: with so many different

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:23.260
channels, streaming services and websites,
media has fragmented -- each appealing to

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.760
their own individual audiences.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:31.270
Fox News for Conservatives, MSNBC for liberals,
and YouTube for flat-earthers.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:35.480
Yet each of these media outlets must pander
to their audience – which is why Fox News

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:41.450
can’t piss off mainstream republicans, evangelicals,
and NRA members and MSNBC can’t piss off

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:45.510
mainstream democrats, progressives, and gun-control
advocates.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.640
The result is isolated bubbles that become
harder and harder to burst.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.800
It’s like Beatty said – offend as few
as possible.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.750
The truth is secondary to comfort.

00:03:55.750 --> 00:04:00.230
And once someone finds their bubble, they’ll
label anything that challenges their ideas

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:02.110
as “fake news.”

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:06.640
Both in the book and in real life, people
protect themselves from any thing that confounds

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:07.640
or upsets.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.560
They just want to continue watching and scrolling
and watching and scrolling and watching and

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:13.570
scrolling…

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:15.890
So what happens next?

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:19.609
In Fahrenheit -- it’s here that the government
steps in, encouraging everyone to stay in

00:04:19.609 --> 00:04:24.160
the bubble and torch any stragglers - sometimes
literally.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.500
Are we destined for a similar fate?

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:31.160
Or will the champions of free thought save us all?

